\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{A global symmetry fixes the mean field}
\date{2026-11-19}

\p{For the \vocabk{orbit weight}{fdip-00ZW} over #{k}, suppose every
site factor is #{G}-invariant. Each site factor is constant on
#{\mathcal O}, and #{\xi(g\phi)=\xi(\phi)}. For any observable #{f}
with values in a #{k}-module, the weighted value of
#{\phi\mapsto f(g\phi)} equals that of #{f}. In particular,
##{\sum_\phi \xi(\phi)\phi_x\in F^G\qquad (x\in X).}
The normalized mean belongs to the same
\vocabk{invariant submodule}{fdip-00WK} when #{Z} is a unit. If
#{F^G=0}, the numerator and every defined mean vanish.}

\proof{
\p{Any two orbit values differ by an element of #{G}, proving
constancy. The diagonal invariance of each edge factor now gives the
weight identity. The global action by #{g} permutes the configuration
set, with inverse given by #{g^{-1}}. Reindexing the sum proves the
observable identity. Evaluation #{\phi\mapsto\phi_x} is equivariant,
so \ref{fdip-00WM} puts its weighted value in #{F^G}. Multiplication
by #{Z^{-1}} preserves this submodule.}
}

\p{The group projection of \ref{fdip-00WP} averages over #{G} by
dividing by #{|G|}. The configuration sum above uses only reindexing.}
